WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.945
Welcome again to another bonus episode of How We Made Your Mother.
欢迎再次收听《如何制作你的老妈》的又一期特别节目。

00:00:03.945 --> 00:00:08.967
It's our final bonus episode before we launch season two towards the end of October.
这是我们在十月下旬推出第二季之前的最后一期特别节目。

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:19.966
In this upcoming season, we've already got a bunch of special guests lined up and a very special announcement about our podcast, which I'm just not quite allowed to announce right now.
在即将到来的这一季中，我们已经安排了一群特邀嘉宾，还有一个关于我们播客的特别公告，目前我还不能直接公布。

00:00:19.966 --> 00:00:21.760
So stay tuned to the internet.
所以请大家密切关注网络上的动态。

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:31.286
This week, we bring you an interview that I did on the new podcast, The Craft of Being, hosted by Anna Schumacher.
本周，我们为你带来一段采访，这是我做客安娜·舒马赫（Anna Schumacher）的新播客《创作的本质》（The Craft of Being）时进行的。

00:00:31.286 --> 00:00:41.145
The craft of being is a podcast via a series of conversations where Anna digs into the question, what the heck even is creativity?
《创作的本质》是一个通过一系列坦诚对话展开的播客，安娜在其中深入探讨了一个问题：究竟什么是创造力？

00:00:41.145 --> 00:00:54.039
I got a chance to talk to her about producing the podcast and working with Josh and Craig and then we go into other projects I've been involved with throughout the years.
我有机会与她聊了聊制作播客的经历、与乔什和克雷格的合作，然后我们还谈到了我多年来参与过的一些其他项目。

00:00:54.039 --> 00:01:02.382
You can listen to the craft of being wherever you get your podcasts and there's even a link in our show notes.
你可以在任何收听播客的地方收听《创作的本质》，我们的节目简介里甚至还有一个链接。

00:01:02.382 --> 00:01:13.000
Also, fun fact, Anna's voice can be heard in the opening montage of Hymmium fan voices in the very first episode of How We Made Your Mother.
另外，趣事分享：安娜的声音出现在了《如何制作你的老妈》第一集片头蒙太奇中那些粉丝的声音里。

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.124
And now, The Craft of Being.
接下来，让我们进入《创作的本质》。

00:01:15.124 --> 00:01:19.978
Hi, hello, and welcome to another episode of The Craft of Being.
嗨，你好，欢迎收听《创作的本质》的又一期节目。

00:01:19.978 --> 00:01:28.472
This is a podcast by way of a series of candid conversations where we dig into what the heck even is creativity.
这是一个通过一系列坦诚对话展开的播客，我们在其中深入探讨究竟什么是创造力。

00:01:28.472 --> 00:01:30.368
Undefined yet definitive.
它既未被定义，却又无比明确。

00:01:30.368 --> 00:01:38.560
We will talk about the types of of innate creative muscles that live within all of us, within you, yes, you.
我们将探讨存在于我们所有人内心的那些与生俱来的创作力量，存在于你——是的，就是你——内心的力量。

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:46.659
And we will talk about how these creative muscles evolve with us across life, work, art, and the pursuit of everything.
我们还将探讨这些创作力量如何在我们的生活、工作、艺术以及对万物的追求中与我们一起进化。

00:01:47.799 --> 00:01:50.959
Today we're gonna chat about film and podcast media.
今天我们要聊的是电影和播客媒体。

00:01:50.959 --> 00:01:59.079
My guest today is well-versed in both, he wears many hats, and our conversation takes us through the systems that support and interact with the artistry.
我今天的嘉宾对这两者都非常精通，他身兼数职，我们的对话将带我们领略那些支持并与艺术创作产生互动的体系。

00:02:00.189 --> 00:02:07.069
Across the board, there's this kind of double consciousness in the way artistic, creative work finds its way into the world.
总体而言，艺术和创意作品走向世界的方式中，存在着一种双重意识。

00:02:07.069 --> 00:02:17.550
On one side, there's the intensity of the creative work itself, the hours of writing, rehearsing, shooting, editing, stitching together details until they finally resolve into something coherent.
一方面，是创作工作本身的强度：数小时的写作、排练、拍摄、剪辑，将细节一点点缝合，直到它们最终凝聚成连贯的作品。

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:27.270
On the other side, there is the preparation, the scaffolding of validation, the decks, the pitches, the budgets, the endless, like me, trust me, gestures.
另一方面，是筹备工作、获得认可的支撑结构、方案书、推介演说、预算，以及无数诸如“相信我，相信我”之类的姿态。

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:32.870
You'll hear Alec talk about that, that are required before many projects can even begin.
你会听到亚历克谈到这一点，这是许多项目启动前所必需的。

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:49.639
And by begin, I mean here, begin with the goal of being seen, being picked up, becoming the thing that launches someone into a place where the work can speak for itself and that someone can keep working in this stream so far as capitalism runs the game, okay?
我说的启动，是指带着被看到、被买下、成为能让某人的作品自己说话并在这个资本主义规则统治的行业里继续工作下去的跳板这一目标的启动，好吧？

00:02:49.639 --> 00:02:55.479
Hollywood, Hollywood, has built an empire on this double consciousness.
好莱坞，好莱坞正是建立在双重意识的帝国之上。

00:02:55.479 --> 00:03:07.879
It is a city where art and commerce are bound together so tightly that they appear inseparable where the beauty of a scene and the pressure of an opening weekend are measured on the same ledger.
这是一座艺术与商业紧密交织、几乎不可分割的城市，在这里，一个场景的美感与首周末票房的压力在同一本账目上被衡量。

00:03:07.879 --> 00:03:20.000
To speak about Hollywood is to speak about an entire architecture of creativity, a system that provides opportunity and exposure, and also dictates form funding and legitimacy.
谈论好莱坞，就是在谈论一整套创造力的建筑学——一个既提供机会与曝光，同时也决定了形式、资金与合法性的系统。

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.039
A film is not simply the work of its director and cast.
一部电影不仅仅是导演和演员的工作。

00:03:24.039 --> 00:03:33.080
It's also the work of financiers, distributors, marketing teams, and executives who determine and whether the work will even see the light of day.
它也是出资人、发行商、营销团队和高管的工作，他们决定着这部作品能否重见天日。

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:35.639
This structure is at once enabling and constraining.
这种结构既是赋能的，也是限制的。

00:03:35.639 --> 00:03:46.719
It allows something as vast and complex as a feature film to even exist, but it also requires the artist to frame their own imagination in ways that appeal to some gatekeepers.
它让像长片这样宏大复杂的作品得以存在，但也要求艺术家用能够吸引某些守门人的方式来构筑自己的想象力。

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:50.759
The Creative Act begins long before a camera rolls, right?
创意行为远在摄像机开拍之前就已经开始了，对吧？

00:03:50.759 --> 00:04:00.699
It begins in the translation of vision into the pitch deck, into the log line, into a concise justification of why the work deserves to exist.
它始于将愿景转化为推介案（pitch deck）、一句话梗概（log line），以及对作品为什么值得存在的简洁论证。

00:04:00.699 --> 00:04:04.759
The podcast world has inherited many of the same dynamics.
播客世界继承了许多相同的动态。

00:04:04.759 --> 00:04:21.588
Just like Tarantino once used to be able to make reservoir dogs in a warehouse on a whisper in a dream, so too can I sit here in our tiny spare room that quadruples as bicycle storage in my desk in a dumping space for outdoor gear and toddler toys and put my thoughts in craft out there.
正如昆汀过去曾能凭着一个梦想的低语，在仓库里拍出《落水狗》一样，我也能坐在这个身兼四职的小备用房间里——它既是自行车储藏室，也是我办公桌的所在地，还是户外装备和幼儿玩具的堆放区——把我的思想和手艺呈现出去。

00:04:21.588 --> 00:04:25.399
Though I don't have Harvey Keitel, and thus it's just you and me.
虽然我没有哈威·凯特尔，所以这里只有你和我。

00:04:25.399 --> 00:04:38.399
What might have once seemed like a freer medium, just pressed record, let the conversation flow, upload it for anyone to hear, has quickly developed its own economies of scale, its own validation structures.
曾经看似更自由的媒介——只需按下录音键，让对话自然流淌，然后上传供任何人收听——现在已经迅速发展出其自身的规模经济和认可结构。

00:04:38.399 --> 00:04:48.399
A podcast needs a host, yes, but it also needs production schedules, advertising partnerships, distribution platforms, and metrics that prove value.
一个播客需要主持人，没错，但它也需要制作日程、广告合作、分发平台以及证明其价值的数据指标。

00:04:48.399 --> 00:04:58.399
Behind every hour of conversation released into the world are countless invisible acts of coordination, emails, calendars, edits, sponsorship copies, social media prompts.
在向世界发布的每一个小时对话背后，都有无数看不见的协调工作：电子邮件、日历、剪辑、赞助文案、社交媒体文案。

00:04:58.399 --> 00:05:05.399
The work disappears into the final product and yet it is essential to the very possibility of the show.
这些工作消失在了最终的产品中，然而它们对于节目能够存在本身却至关重要。

00:05:05.399 --> 00:05:09.399
To be clear, I am all of the roles and we do not have a sponsorship department.
需要说明的是，我一人兼顾了所有角色，我们并没有专门的赞助部门。

00:05:09.399 --> 00:05:15.709
There is a paradox of much of modern artistic creativity.
现代艺术创作中存在着这样一个悖论。

00:05:15.709 --> 00:05:20.709
The most ephemeral media often require the most relentless scaffolding.
最瞬息万变的媒介往往需要最不懈的支撑结构。

00:05:20.709 --> 00:05:25.709
Episodes vanish into a stream, but the labor that sustains them is constant.
单集节目消失在信息流中，但维持它们运转的劳作却是恒常的。

00:05:25.709 --> 00:05:31.709
Movies may be remembered for decades, but before they can exist, they must pass through this obstacle course.
电影可能会被人们铭记几十年，但在它们得以存在之前，必须通过这道障碍赛。

00:05:31.709 --> 00:05:39.709
In both cases for film and podcasting, the act of creation is inseparable from the structures that allow it to reach an audience.
无论是电影还是播客，创作行为都与让它触及受众的结构密不可分。

00:05:39.709 --> 00:05:45.709
There's something instructive in noticing how creators move between these systems.
观察创作者如何在这些系统之间穿梭，具有某种启发意义。

00:05:45.709 --> 00:05:52.949
Directing a film and producing a podcast may seem like entirely different crafts yet they share a common rhythm.
执导电影和制作播客似乎是完全不同的手艺，但它们却有着共同的节奏。

00:05:52.949 --> 00:06:01.990
Establish the outline, the framework, set the stage, line up the ducks, and then let the unpredictable alchemy of collaboration bring something to life.
建立大纲、框架，搭建舞台，把事情安排妥当，然后让不可预测的合作炼金术将某些东西带入现实。

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:06.430
A director can block a scene, but the actors will always bring their own nuance.
导演可以为场景走位，但演员总会带来他们自己的细微差别。

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:14.230
A producer can schedule the recording, but the conversation will always unfold in ways that no outline can fully anticipate.
制作人可以安排录音时间，但对话的展开方式永远是任何大纲都无法完全预料的。

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:22.160
Creativity, as we've heard on many of these episodes, so often thrives at the junction of order and play.
正如我们在许多节目中听到的一样，创造力往往在秩序与游戏的交汇处蓬勃发展。

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:26.000
In today's conversation, we get into the way projects are set up.
在今天的对话中，我们深入探讨了项目的筹备方式。

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:33.720
Spreadsheets, fun spreadsheets, sequences of scenes ordered and reordered, and then the joy of stepping back to watch collaborators bring the unexpected.
电子表格，有趣的电子表格，按顺序排列又重新排序的场景序列，然后是退后一步，看着合作伙伴带来意想不到惊喜的喜悦。

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:42.519
And what emerges is the broader truth about film, television, podcasting, creativity itself, that it oscillates between the structure and flow.
由此浮现的是关于电影、电视、播客以及创造力本身的更广泛真理：它在结构与流动之间摇摆。

00:06:42.519 --> 00:06:48.199
Too much order, the work becomes lifeless, too much freedom, the work risks in coherence.
秩序太多，作品就会变得毫无生气；自由太多，作品则面临逻辑不清的风险。

00:06:48.199 --> 00:06:49.199
This is not a lament.
这不是一种哀叹。

00:06:49.199 --> 00:06:54.800
It is a recognition that modern creativity is always entangled with its systems of distribution and visibility.
这是一种认同，即现代创造力总是与其分发和曝光系统纠缠在一起。

00:06:54.800 --> 00:07:02.759
To make something is also to navigate the infrastructures that allow it to be seen, heard, or streamed, experienced.
创作某物，同时也意味着驾驭那些让它得以被看到、被听到、被流媒体播放或被体验的基础设施。

00:07:02.759 --> 00:07:08.689
here today that my guest, Alec, reflects that this negotiation is not a distraction from his creativity.
今天在这里，我的嘉宾亚历克反思道，这种协商并不是对他创造力的分散。

00:07:08.689 --> 00:07:09.659
It is creativity.
这就是创造力本身。

00:07:09.659 --> 00:07:18.439
To build a schedule that makes weekly episodes possible to reorder sequences in a screenplay until they lock into place, to coordinate your collaborators.
构建使每周播客成为可能的时间表，在剧本中重新排序场景直到它们锁定到位，协调你的合作伙伴。

00:07:18.439 --> 00:07:21.146
These are acts of invention.
这些都是发明的行为。

00:07:21.146 --> 00:07:28.300
So to hear him in his own words, without further ado, welcome to the show.
废话不多说，让我们听听他自己的肺腑之言，欢迎来到本节目。

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:29.266
Thank you.
谢谢。

00:07:29.266 --> 00:07:30.136
Baseball.
棒球。

00:07:30.136 --> 00:07:32.746
I'm talking about baseball.
我指的是棒球。

00:07:32.746 --> 00:07:34.873
You've been called up.
你被征召了。

00:07:34.873 --> 00:07:41.350
You're I've been taught, I'm a put me in coach, I am ready to play.
我被告知，我是那种“教练请让我上场，我已经准备好比赛了”的人。

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:45.350
Excellent, well please introduce yourself, what's your name?
太好了，那么请自我介绍一下，你叫什么名字？

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:49.800
What do you do and how do we know each other?
你是做什么的？我们是怎么认识的？

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:55.800
My name is Alec Lev, I am a director and a producer, I'm going to go with that.
我叫亚历克·列夫（Alec Lev），我是一名导演兼制作人，我倾向于这么称呼自己。

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:05.608
And we know each other, well we met twice, I would say at least, we in other lives definitely passed and vaguely present for me.
至于我们怎么认识的，嗯，我们至少见过两次面，在其他人生阶段我们肯定有过交集，对我来说有些依稀模糊但确实存在。

00:08:05.608 --> 00:08:12.888
We are we're sign language interpreters and we interpret it at the Marlton School for the Deaf.
我们曾是手语翻译，我们在马尔顿聋哑学校（Marlton School for the Deaf）担任翻译。

00:08:12.888 --> 00:08:28.981
I say this with I was gonna say great confidence with near confidence but it should be known that years later you through a mutual friend you hired me to direct some public service videos, I think we call them.
我本想说我对此有着十足的信心，其实是差不多的信心，但大家应该知道，多年后你通过一位共同的朋友雇我导演了一些公共服务视频，我想我们当时是这么叫它们的。

00:08:28.981 --> 00:08:37.333
And I don't remember exactly but the meeting probably went something like where I said, hi, nice to meet you.
我不记得具体细节了，但那次会面大概是这样的情景：我说，嗨，很高兴认识你。

00:08:37.333 --> 00:08:40.858
And you said something like you idiot, we met.
而你说：你个白痴，我们早就认识了。

00:08:40.858 --> 00:08:44.460
I think we called them tone poems at one point.
我想我们在某个阶段把它们叫做音诗（tone poems）。

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:50.054
Can you expand a little bit on what it is that you produce and or direct?
你能详细谈谈你制作或导演的内容吗？

00:08:50.054 --> 00:08:50.554
Sure.
当然。

00:08:50.554 --> 00:09:01.742
So as we speak, I am producing the podcast How We Made Your Mother, which is a rewatch podcast of the hit television show, How We Met Your Mother.
录制这期节目时，我正在制作播客《如何制作你的老妈》，这是一档重温热播电视剧《老爸老妈的浪漫史》的播客。

00:09:01.742 --> 00:09:05.267
I can't even say the name of the show anymore.
我现在甚至都说不对这部剧的名字了。

00:09:05.267 --> 00:09:08.486
How I met your mother, that's the TV show.
《老爸老妈的浪漫史》，那是这部电视剧。

00:09:08.486 --> 00:09:20.976
And our podcast is with The Eye and How I Met Your Mother, Josh Radner, and the co-creator of the show, Craig Thomas, with whom I went to Wesleyan back in the day.
我们的播客嘉宾有乔什·拉德纳（Josh Radnor）——剧中的“我”——以及该剧的联合创作者克雷格·托马斯（Craig Thomas），我和他当年在卫斯理大学（Wesleyan）是校友。

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:53.807
so we've been great friends for decades and I worked on the show for a little bit back when it was on and so I'm producing that now and I've produced a couple of other podcasts meeting Tom Cruise, people telling insane stories about how they met Tom Cruise and as a director I have done a lot of theater I was a theater major like you I was a theater major back in college and I've done a bunch of directly mostly with deaf theaters.
所以我们是几十年的好朋友，当年该剧播出时我也参与过制作，所以我现在在制作这个播客。我还制作过其他几档播客，比如《遇见汤姆·克鲁斯》（meeting Tom Cruise），讲述人们如何偶遇汤姆·克鲁斯的疯狂故事。作为一名导演，我做过很多戏剧，我大学时的主修专业是戏剧，就像你一样，我大学时也是戏剧专业，我导演过很多作品，主要是与聋哑剧团合作。

00:09:53.807 --> 00:09:56.166
I guess we can talk about that.
我想我们可以聊聊这个。

00:09:56.166 --> 00:10:01.112
And then I directed a couple of films, one of which you produced.
然后我还导演了几部电影，其中有一部是你担任制作人的。

00:10:01.112 --> 00:10:10.700
And you and I, as of this recording, are gunning towards the next collaboration that I will direct and you will produce again.
截至录制时，你和我正准备开启下一次合作，我将担任导演，而你将再次担任制作人。

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:16.860
Then the question I always ask everyone is, do you feel like a creative person?
那么我经常问大家的一个问题是：你觉得自己是一个有创造力的人吗？

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:17.666
I better.
我最好是。

00:10:17.666 --> 00:10:18.166
Yes.
是的。

00:10:18.166 --> 00:10:21.480
I have no caveats to that whatsoever.
对此我没有任何保留条件。

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:31.690
Yes, that is the only thing I'm interested in doing professionally and I don't even call it professionally really.
是的，这是我职业生涯中唯一感兴趣的事情，甚至连“职业生涯”这个词我都觉得不太准确。

